     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg per day. The usual maximum is 2,000 to 2,550 mg per day, depending on the formulation, so this is about double that. An overdose raises the risk of lactic acidosis, which can be life-threatening. A single 1,700 mg dose is also above the typical maximum for one administration, which is about 1,000 mg. This may be a typo, for example 850 mg three times daily (2,550 mg per day) or 1,700 mg total per day.\n\n2. **You aren't a clinician.** Medication orders should come from, or be verified by, the prescriber responsible for the patient. I can't confirm that this order was actually intended or authorized.\n\nPlease ask the prescriber or a pharmacist to confirm the intended dose. If the prescriber gives me a corrected order, I can enter it, and I'd still want it to come from them."
      }
     ],
